  realLife: [
    { tag: 'INTEGRITY', tagZh: '正直', verseRef: 'Proverbs 19:1',
      titleZh: '当诚实要付出代价', titleEn: 'When honesty has a price',
      bodyZh: '“行为纯正的贫穷人，胜过乖谬愚妄的富足人”（19:1）。生活里常有这样的时刻：说个谎、做点手脚，就能多赚一点、少麻烦一点；坚持诚实，反而要吃亏。箴言把这两种人排了个出人意料的序：宁可穷而正直，也不要富而虚假。这不是贬低努力赚钱，而是提醒：当利益和诚实发生冲突时，别把身上最值钱的东西——你的品格——卖掉。',
      bodyEn: '“Better is the poor who walks in his integrity than he who is perverse in his lips and is a fool” (19:1). Life keeps handing us moments where a lie or a little maneuvering would earn more or save trouble, while staying honest costs us. Proverbs ranks these two people in a surprising order: better poor and honest than rich and false. This isn’t knocking hard-earned money; it’s a reminder that when gain and honesty collide, don’t sell off the most valuable thing you own — your character.' },
    { tag: 'PATIENCE', tagZh: '忍耐', verseRef: 'Proverbs 19:11',
      titleZh: '选择越过一个冒犯', titleEn: 'Choosing to let an offense go',
      bodyZh: '“宽恕人的过失便是自己的荣耀”（19:11）。有人无心（或有心）冒犯了你，你可以纠缠、报复、记在心里，也可以选择“越过它”。箴言说，后者不是懦弱，而是荣耀——是有见识之人才有的度量。这不是说所有伤害都要装作没发生（有些事需要认真处理），而是说：不必为每一句刺耳的话、每一个小小的失礼都停下来开战。放过一件小事，往往比赢下它更让你自由。',
      bodyEn: '“It is his glory to overlook an offense” (19:11). Someone slights you, carelessly or on purpose, and you can dwell on it, retaliate, and file it away — or you can choose to let it go. Proverbs says the latter is not cowardice but glory, the largeness only the discerning possess. This doesn’t mean pretending every hurt never happened (some things do need to be addressed); it means you needn’t go to war over every sharp word or small discourtesy. Letting a small thing pass often frees you more than winning it would.' },
    { tag: 'GENEROSITY', tagZh: '慷慨', verseRef: 'Proverbs 19:17',
      titleZh: '善待穷人，神必偿还', titleEn: 'Kindness to the poor, repaid by God',
      bodyZh: '“怜悯贫穷的，就是借给耶和华；他的善行，耶和华必偿还”（19:17）。这是一个大胆的应许：当你帮助一个无力回报你的人，神把这笔账记在自己名下，并且承诺亲自偿还。这意味着，慷慨从来不是白白流失，即使受助的人永远不会、也无法报答你。今天你可以为某个有需要的人做一件具体的小事——一顿饭、一次陪伴、一笔奉献——相信神看见，也必记念。',
      bodyEn: '“He who has pity on the poor lends to the LORD; he will reward him” (19:17). It’s a bold promise: when you help someone who can’t pay you back, God puts the debt in his own name and pledges to repay it personally. That means generosity is never simply drained away, even when the person you help never can or will return the favor. Today you can do one concrete thing for someone in need — a meal, your presence, a gift — trusting that God sees and will remember.' },
    { tag: 'LISTENING', tagZh: '受教', verseRef: 'Proverbs 19:20',
      titleZh: '智慧是一生学来的', titleEn: 'Wisdom is learned over a lifetime',
      bodyZh: '“你要听劝教，受训诲，使你终久有智慧”（19:20）。这节经文提醒我们，智慧不是某天突然开窍，而是一年又一年肯听、肯学、肯被纠正累积出来的。这需要一点谦卑：承认自己还在学，别人可能看得比我清楚。今天有没有一个你一直不太想听的建议、或一位你有点抵触的人的提醒？也许正是在那里，藏着你现在最需要的成长。',
      bodyEn: '“Listen to counsel and receive instruction, that you may be wise in your latter end” (19:20). This verse reminds us wisdom isn’t a sudden flash of insight but the accumulation of years of listening, learning, and being corrected. It takes some humility: admitting you’re still learning and that others may see more clearly than you do. Is there a piece of advice you’ve been reluctant to hear, or a person whose input you resist? That may be exactly where the growth you most need is hiding.' },
    { tag: 'CONTENTMENT', tagZh: '知足', verseRef: 'Proverbs 19:23',
      titleZh: '知足从敬畏神而来', titleEn: 'Contentment that comes from fearing God',
      bodyZh: '“敬畏耶和华的，得着生命；他必恒久知足，不遭祸患”（19:23）。我们常以为知足来自“终于拥有得够多了”，但箴言把知足的根扎在别处——扎在与神的关系里。当一个人真正敬畏、信靠神，他的安稳就不再随着收入、评价、境遇上下起伏。这不是说从此没有难处，而是说心里有一份不被夺去的踏实。今天试着数算你已经拥有的，让感恩把敬畏神化成实实在在的知足。',
      bodyEn: '“The fear of the LORD leads to life, then contentment; he rests and will not be touched by trouble” (19:23). We often assume contentment comes from “finally having enough,” but Proverbs roots it elsewhere — in a relationship with God. When someone truly reveres and trusts God, their security stops rising and falling with income, reviews, and circumstances. This doesn’t mean there’s no more hardship, but that there is an inner steadiness that can’t be taken away. Today, try counting what you already have, and let gratitude turn reverence for God into real contentment.' },
  ],

